            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="true" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <HouseholdOperatingConditions numberOfOccupants="3" coolingSeasonMonths="0" isClothesWasherEnergySaver="true" isLightingEnergySaver="true" areAppliancesEnergySaver="true">
                <Thermostat heatingDaytime="17" heatingNighttime="17" cooling="25" />
            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. 

1 - Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls. There are electrical outlets gaskets available to help seal these areas up. The gasket is simply installed over top of the outlet, and behind the plastic cover plate. I recommend you install on of these on any electrical outlets (or light switches) on an exterior wall of the home. 

2 - Air leakage was also observed through the two bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model. As we discussed, a newer ENERGY STARÂ® bathroom exhaust fan will have a built in butterfly damper to better seal when not in use. 

3 - There was quite a bit of leakage observed around the attic hatch, and was confirmed by the build up of moisture (and ice) around the access hatch when it was opened. I recommend to seal the access hatch using 'Draft Attack' caulking to seal up the access hatch. This is a removable caulking should you need to open the access hatch for any reason. 

4 - The back door appears to be warped and does not contact the weather stripping well, paticularily in the corners. I would recommend replacing this door and the weather stripping along with it. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 80&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. I think you could achieve greater than10% improvement for your home. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;







&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 240&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

















&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house was estimated to have R28 of blown cellulose and fiberglass batt insulation. As you mentioned a roof replacement is in your future plans, I have modelled bringing the insulation value up to R50.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By increasing the insulation to R50 in the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 130&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 125 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with 2x4 framing with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modeled adding 2 inches (R10) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene insulation to the 2x4 walls. 



In conjuction with the exterior wall insulation upgrade section noted above, I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation to the above grade portion of the basement walls.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 2 inches (R10) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the 2x4 framed basement walls, as well as, 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, to the above grade portion of the basement walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 400&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;13.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 625 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</foundation>
                <windows>

Overall your windows appear to be in good shape. As we discussed, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading the front window by the front door and the window by the back door with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. If you are considering replacing any windows, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or picture windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing the two windows listed above with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, picture, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 50&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

During my assessment, there appeared to be sufficient ventilation for your home as it currently is. 



&lt;b&gt;NOTE:&lt;/b&gt; By achieving significant air sealing results, the current ventilation of the home may no longer be enough to provide enough fresh air for the home. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



I recommend that you have an ENERGY STARÂ® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI. An HRV system could be designed into your current furnace ducting design, minimizing the installation requirements. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 151 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly.

If you decide to consider a boiler for your heating, as described in heating upgrade 'Option 2' above, it would be ideal to use your boiler to heat your hot water via a dedicated loop, and connect this loop to the hot water tank to prevent the boiler from "short cycling".



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your boiler to heat your hot water, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,000&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Option 1: High-Efficiency Condensing Propane Furnace&lt;/b&gt;

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 83% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 900&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;27.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;b&gt;Option 2: High Efficiency Condensing Propane Boiler&lt;/b&gt;

Another option you could consider is upgrading to a high efficiency condensing propane boiler. A fan coil could be used to transfer heat from the boiler into the exisiting duct work of the home. As you are planning extensive renovations to basement, in-floor heating could also be an option. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



If you decide to consider a boiler for your heating, please see the section below for an option regarding your domestic hot water. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 860&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;26.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $5.67 per bag



